at the code, `JSObject` structures have the following relevant fields:\n- `class_id`: determines the object type\n- `u.typed_array`: for TypedArray objects, contains buffer pointer and length\n- `u.func`: for function objects, contains bytecode and other execution data\n\nCorrupting a TypedArray's buffer pointer is safer than corrupting function objects directly, as it gives us controlled read/write without immediate crashes.\n\n### Arbitrary Read/Write via TypedArray Corruption\nIf we can overwrite a TypedArray's `buffer` pointer and `byte_length`, we can:\n- Read arbitrary memory by accessing the array elements\n- Write arbitrary memory by setting array elements\n\nThis is a powerful primitive that can lead to full code execution.\n\n## Next Steps\n\n1. Create a reliable heap grooming primitive\n2. Implement the buffer overflow to corrupt a nearby TypedArray\n3. Develop arbitrary read/write using the corrupted TypedArray\n4. Use this to achieve code execution and spawn a shell"})
